<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  A one-shot: fire once, spawn one supervisor per cut-off Run, exit. Nothing is resident and
  nothing watches anything (ADR-0011).

  **It fires at login, not at boot, and that is as early as darwin goes.** `launchctl bootstrap
  gui/$(id -u)` puts the job in the GUI domain, so `RunAtLoad` fires when that domain loads. A
  restarted Mac therefore sits with its cut-off Runs un-re-entered until a human logs in. The
  linux unit beside this one does reach boot, with `loginctl enable-linger`; darwin has no
  equivalent shape:

    - A LaunchDaemon fires before login, but it resolves root's `$HOME` and so a different
      `~/.grind` — and `$HOME` is the only variable Grind reads, with no override (ADR-0008).
    - Setting `HOME` on a LaunchDaemon does not rescue it either. On a FileVault Mac — the
      default — `/Users/<user>` is not decrypted until someone unlocks at the login window, so
      `~/.grind/runs/` does not exist yet to be read. There is nothing to re-enter from.

  So *survives a restart* here means **survives a restart plus a login**, and `grind doctor` says
  so in its satisfied text: `launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/…` can only run from inside a
  logged-in GUI session, which is the very condition it would need to tell the two apart, so it
  structurally cannot check this and does not pretend to.

  Install as a **user** agent, so `$HOME` resolves to the dispatching user's:

      cp dist/launchd/com.grind.resume-all.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
      launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.grind.resume-all.plist

  A plist copied into place and never bootstrapped is not loaded, and it fails one reboot later
  with a Run stranded — which is why `grind doctor` checks for *loaded* rather than *present*.

  `AbandonProcessGroup` is the load-bearing key. Without it launchd reaps the whole process group
  when the job exits, and this job's entire purpose is to spawn detached supervisors and exit —
  so every re-entered Run would die seconds after login, silently. It is the launchd half of the
  systemd unit's `KillMode=process`.

  **No `StandardOutPath` / `StandardErrorPath`.** They held `/tmp/grind-resume-all.log`, and
  `/tmp` on macOS is mode 1777: launchd opens those keys with `O_CREAT|O_APPEND` as the
  dispatching user and follows symlinks, so another local account could pre-plant one and
  redirect the append into a file the grind user owns. The certain half was disclosure — the file
  is world-readable and named every cut-off run id and every skipped Run's full worktree path.
  Nothing else in the repo places state outside `~/.grind/` (ADR-0008), and nothing is lost:
  every line this job prints is also appended to the relevant
  `~/.grind/runs/<run-id>/supervisor.log`, and `launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.grind.resume-all`
  reports its exit status. The systemd unit has never had log keys either.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.grind.resume-all</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/local/bin/grind</string>
    <string>resume</string>
    <string>--all</string>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
